e when Local + current-branch was selected. Now a user in detached HEAD (common mid-bisect, mid-rebase, after checking out a SHA) can no longer use `entire dispatch` interactively at all, even for the Cloud path where the current branch is never referenced. Fix: store `(branch string, branchErr error)` on `state`, don't fail in `runDispatchWizard`, and only surface the error inside `state.resolve()` when `isLocal() && !AllBranches` — matching the exact condition under which `ResolveOptions` actually invokes the callback.\n\n- **[cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/mode_local.go:301-315](cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/mode_local.go:301) — `resolveRepoFullName` now drops underlying error context.** The three rewrites replace `fmt.Errorf(\"find origin remote: %w\", err)` with three hardcoded strings that no longer preserve the wrapped error. If `repo.Remote(\"origin\")` fails for a non-\"not found\" reason (I/O error, permission error on .git/config, malformed config), the user sees the misleading `\"dispatch currently supports GitHub repositories with an origin remote\"`. The user-facing message is fine for the common case, but please add a `logging.Warn(ctx, ..., \"error\", err)` alongside — mirrors what `doJSON` now does in cloud.go. Alternatively, `fmt.Errorf(\"<friendly message>: %w\", err)` and let the wrapped error land in the CLI error stream.\n\n- **[cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/mode_local.go:157-165](cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/mode_local.go:157) — two full-repo `git log --grep=Entire-Checkpoint:` scans per dispatch.** After the fix, `enumerateRepoCandidates` now unconditionally invokes `loadCommitSubjectsByCheckpoint` (scans `--all --since --grep`) in addition to the conditional `reachableCheckpointIDsInRange` scan (scans `base..HEAD --since --grep`) when `ImplicitCurrentBranch` is set. These are two separate traversals of similar commit sets with identical grep patterns. Since both return `map[checkpointID]...` structures, they could be collapsed into a single `git log` that returns `SUBJECT\\x00BODY\\x00COMMIT_HASH\\x00\\x00` and the caller can decide reachability by intersecting the set with a cheaper `git rev-list base..HEAD` lookup (or by an extra `%d` format flag for ref decorations). Not urgent since we moved from O(N) to O(2) scans, but worth flagging: `loadCommitSubjectsByCheckpoint` is called even when there are zero in-window checkpoints (the function doesn't early-exit).\n\n### Suggestions (Consider)\n\n- **[cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/mode_local.go:352-390](cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/mode_local.go:352) — `loadCommitSubjectsByCheckpoint` silently tolerates commits where `git log` body parsing is ambiguous.** The format is `%s\\x00%B\\x00\\x00` and git separates records with an additional `\\n`. The parser handles the trailing `\\n` via `TrimSpace(parts[0])`, but if a commit subject itself contained a literal NUL byte (impossible in practice, git rejects NUL in commit messages — but worth a comment explaining why the NUL delimiter is safe). Adding a one-line comment above the function documenting the invariant (\"git rejects NUL bytes in commit messages, so NUL is a safe record delimiter\") would pay for itself in six months.\n\n- **[cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/generate.go:194-196](cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/generate.go:194) — `sanitizeDispatchVoice` is now a redundant wrapper.** `sanitizeDispatchVoice(raw)` is identical to `sanitizeDispatchPromptString(raw)`. Kept for one external caller at `generate.go:222` and one test at `generate_test.go:111`. Consider either inlining or deleting the alias in a follow-up once the test is updated.\n\n- **[cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/cloud.go:181](cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/cloud.go:181) — transport-level error still formats method + path into user-visible text.** The trimmed-message cleanup only covered the HTTP-status branch. For network-level errors (DNS failure, connection refused, TLS handshake), the user still sees `\"POST /api/v1/dispatches/generate: Post \\\"https://...\\\": dial tcp: connection refused\"`. Consider the same split: warn-log the method/path, return `fmt.Errorf(\"dispatch service unreachable: %w\", err)`. Not worth a separate commit if the rest is fine, but would complete the consistency story.\n\n- **[cmd/entire/cli/dispatch_wizard.go:229](cmd/entire/cli/dispatch_wizard.go:229) — removing `go loadRepos()` makes Cloud users perceive a freeze on the repo picker.** On a slow network or rate-limited `gh api`, the picker will render blocked. Consider re-introducing the prefetch but gated behind `state.modeChoice == dispatchWizardModeServer` (set once the first group completes). A `sync.Once` already protects the loader from double-execution. This is a polish follow-up, not a blocker.\n\n- **[cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/options.go:10](cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/options.go:10) — `githubRepoSlugPattern` is a package-scope compiled regex but is only used in `validateRepoSlugs`.** `regexp.MustCompile` at package init is fine but obscures the fact that the pattern is GitHub-specific. If `--repos` later supports non-GitHub hosts (GitLab, Gitea) the name will mislead. Consider `dispatchRepoSlugPattern` or `repoOwnerSlashRepoPattern`.\n\n### Praise\n\n- **Accessibility fix is tight and well-tested.** The test stubs `runInteractiveDispatch` AND `renderTerminalMarkdown` to explicitly `t.Fatal` if either fires in `ACCESSIBLE=1` mode. That's the right shape — catches both the TUI and the Glamour renderer, so a future refactor that moves Glamour into a new path will trip the regression pin.\n\n- **Branch-resolution caching preserves the functional contract.** `state.resolve()` still receives a `func() (string, error)` at [dispatch_wizard.go:109-111](cmd/entire/cli/dispatch_wizard.go:109), so the `dispatch.ResolveOptions` signature didn't have to change and the cloud-mode path (which never calls the callback) is unaffected. The only real bug is the eager-failure in the wizard bootstrap, not the shape of the caching fix.\n\n- **`loadCommitSubjectsByCheckpoint` uses `trailers.ParseAllCheckpoints`.** Re-using the canonical trailer parser (instead of a bespoke regex) is the right call — and the same parser is used by `reachableCheckpointIDsInRange`, keeping the two scans semantically aligned. If the trailer format evolves, both scans update in lockstep.\n\n- **Prompt sanitization extension is uniform.** Every user-controlled string that ends up in the LLM prompt (title, covered repos, repo full name, section label, bullet text, source, branch, labels) is run through the same sanitizer. `CheckpointID` and `CreatedAt` are left alone, which is correct — they're programmatic, not user-controlled.\n\n- **Cloud error simplification has a pin test.** `TestCloudClient_CreateDispatch_EscapesErrorBody` now asserts both the new error-string shape (`\"dispatch service returned status 502\"`) and the absence of `/api/v1/dispatches/generate` in user-facing text. Both the positive and negative assertion pattern is solid.\n\n## Summary\n\nThree of the four commits are high-quality, minimal, well-tested fixes to review-02's feedback. The accessibility, validation, and sanitization commits are merge-ready.\n\nThe branch-caching commit (`19217f27b`) over-corrects: it resolved the per-keystroke git fan-out but introduced a new blocker for detached-HEAD Cloud users (who previously reached the wizard and now can't). The fix is small — gate the error surfacing on the actual usage condition in `state.resolve()`, not on wizard bootstrap. Please address before merging.\n\nSecondary concerns: `resolveRepoFullName` now hides underlying error context (recommend `logging.Warn`-and-wrap), and `enumerateRepoCandidates` now issues two full-repo `git log --grep` scans per dispatch (the first commit fixed N+1 by moving to O(1) subjects; the second scan for reachability is old, but consolidation is worth a follow-up).\n\nNo evidence of new token/prompt leakage. No regressions in HTTPS enforcement or default-branch ancestry (those tests are unchanged and still pass). `mise run lint` was not run per scope constraints; suggest the author verify before push.\n\nEstimated effort to address: 30-60 minutes for the detached-HEAD regression + 10 minutes for `resolveRepoFullName` logging. The dual-`git log` consolidation can be a separate PR.\n"}}
